\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  Different monotonic increase of the total fraction of students in the learned state over iteration number. The blue colored plots are for TI while other colors are for PI. The same color corresponds to the seating arrangement (SA) used. Each PI's SA corresponds to a different color: orange (for inner corner), green (outer corner), orange (center), and pink (random). The line width is proportional to the $N$ used. Other pertinent parameters fixed: $\rho _0=0.5$ and $\delta \!\lambda =0.2$. The bands around each line show the standard deviation of the data after $20$ independent random trials. Higher fraction of learned students indicate better overall learning. (a)\nobreakspace  {}Plot for different class sizes $N$. The best performant SA (Inner Corner) is used for comparison. (b)\nobreakspace  {}Plot for different seating arrangements (SAs). }}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison size}{{3}{4}{Different monotonic increase of the total fraction of students in the learned state over iteration number. The blue colored plots are for TI while other colors are for PI. The same color corresponds to the seating arrangement (SA) used. Each PI's SA corresponds to a different color: orange (for inner corner), green (outer corner), orange (center), and pink (random). The line width is proportional to the $N$ used. Other pertinent parameters fixed: $\rho _0=0.5$ and $\delta \!\lambda =0.2$. The bands around each line show the standard deviation of the data after $20$ independent random trials. Higher fraction of learned students indicate better overall learning. (a)~Plot for different class sizes $N$. The best performant SA (Inner Corner) is used for comparison. (b)~Plot for different seating arrangements (SAs)}{figure.3}{}}
